hadiths[477]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4771","Daghfal ibn Hanzalah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning what happened to the fasting prescribed upon the Christians. > **""The Christians had the fasting of the month of Ramadan upon them. They had a king who fell ill, and he said: if Allah heals him, he will surely add ten days. Then there was a king after him who ate meat and fell ill, and he said: if Allah heals him, he will surely add eight days. Then there was a king after him who said: it is not complete with these days that we should complete them and make our fasting in spring, so they became fifty days.""**"
"4772","Abu Umamah al-Bahili (may Allah be pleased with him) heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explaining the obligation of fasting and the status of night prayer, and the lesson from the Children of Israel. > **""Indeed Allah has made the fasting of Ramadan obligatory upon you, and He has not made its standing in prayer obligatory upon you. Its standing is a thing you have newly taken up, so persist in it. For some people from the Children of Israel innovated an innovation, then Allah blamed them for abandoning it, for He said: {And monasticism, they innovated it; We did not prescribe it upon them except seeking the pleasure of Allah} [al-Hadid: 27], to the end of the verse.""**"
"4773","Jabir ibn Abdillah (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described three wretched ones. > **""Whoever reaches Ramadan and does not fast it is wretched. Whoever reaches his two parents or one of them and does not treat them kindly is wretched. And whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings upon me is wretched.""**"
"4774","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that when Rajab entered, the Prophet ﷺ would supplicate for blessing and for reaching Ramadan. > **""O Allah, bless us in Rajab and Sha'ban, and convey us to Ramadan.""**"
"4775","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the rank of Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijjah. > **""The master of the months is the month of Ramadan, and the greatest of them in sanctity is Dhu al-Hijjah.""**"
"4776","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed of the best in each category. > **""Shall I not inform you of the best of the angels? Jibril, peace be upon him. And the best of the Prophets? Adam. And the best of the days? The day of Friday. And the best of the months? The month of Ramadan. And the best of the nights? The Night of Decree. And the best of the women? Maryam, daughter of Imran.""**"
"4777","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said that the master of the months is the month of Ramadan, and the master of the days is the day of Friday."
"4778","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described five qualities given to his Ummah in Ramadan, and explained when their forgiveness occurs. > **""My Ummah has been given five qualities in Ramadan which no nation before them was given: the breath of the fasting person is sweeter with Allah than the scent of musk; the fish seek forgiveness for them until they break their fast; Allah, Mighty and Majestic, adorns His Paradise every day, then says: My righteous servants are about to cast off from themselves the burden and harm and come to you; the rebellious devils are chained therein, so they do not reach therein what they used to reach in other than it; and they are forgiven in the last night."" It was said: O Messenger of Allah, is it the Night of Decree? He said: ""No, but the worker is only paid his wage when he completes his work.""**"
"4779","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore an oath concerning how Ramadan differs for Muslims and hypocrites. > **""No month has come upon the Muslims better for them than Ramadan, and no month has come upon the hypocrites worse for them than Ramadan. That is because the believers prepare therein strength for worship, and the hypocrites prepare therein heedlessness of people and their faults. It is booty for the believers which the wicked one loses.""**"
"4780","In another narration of the preceding report, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained what Allah records before the month is entered. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, surely records his reward and his voluntary deeds before He brings him into it, and He records his wretchedness before He brings him into it.""**"
"4781","Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) heard the Prophet ﷺ speak when the crescent of Ramadan had appeared, and a man from Khuza'ah asked him to narrate more, so he described Paradise and the reward of the fasting person in detail. > **""If the servants knew what is in Ramadan, my Ummah would wish that the whole year were Ramadan. Indeed Paradise is adorned for Ramadan from the beginning of the year to the next year. When the first day of Ramadan comes, a wind blows from beneath the Throne and the leaves of Paradise rustle. The wide-eyed Houris look at that and say: O Lord, appoint for us in this month husbands from Your servants whose eyes are cooled by them and whose eyes are cooled by us. There is no servant who fasts Ramadan except that he is married to a wife from the wide-eyed Houris in a tent of hollow pearl, of that which Allah described: {Houris, secluded in tents} [al-Rahman: 72]. Upon each woman among them are seventy robes, no robe of which is of the colour of another. She is given seventy kinds of perfume, no kind of which is of the scent of another. For each woman among them are seventy beds of red ruby, decorated with pearls. Upon each bed are seventy spreads whose linings are of istabraq, and above the seventy spreads are seventy couches. For each woman among them are seventy thousand maidservants for her needs, and seventy thousand manservants, with each manservant a plate of gold wherein is a kind of food, finding in its last morsel a delight not found in its first. Her husband is given the like of that upon a bed of red ruby, upon him two bracelets of gold decorated with red ruby. This is for each day he fasted Ramadan, besides what he did of good deeds.""**"
"4782","Abu Mas'ud al-Ghifari (may Allah be pleased with him) heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak when the month of Ramadan had appeared, and a man from Khuza'ah asked him to narrate, so he described the adornment of Paradise and the daily marriage to Houris. > **""If the servants knew what is in the month of Ramadan, the servants would wish that the month of Ramadan were a whole year. Indeed Paradise is adorned for the month of Ramadan from the beginning of the year to the beginning of the next year. When the first night comes, a wind blows from beneath the Throne and the leaves of Paradise rustle. The wide-eyed Houris look at that and say: O Lord, appoint for us in this month husbands from Your servants whose eyes are cooled by them and whose eyes are cooled by us. There is no servant who fasts the month of Ramadan except that Allah marries him each day to a wife from the wide-eyed Houris in a tent of hollow pearl, of that with which Allah described the wide-eyed Houris, secluded in tents. Upon each woman among them are seventy robes, none of which is of the colour of another. She is given seventy kinds of perfume, none of which resembles another. Each woman among them is upon a bed of ruby decorated with pearls, upon seventy spreads whose linings are of istabraq, and above the seventy spreads are seventy couches. For each woman among them are seventy attendants for her service, and seventy to meet her husband, with each attendant a plate of gold wherein is a kind of food, finding in its last the same delight as in its first. Her husband is given the like of that upon a bed of red ruby, upon him two bracelets of gold decorated with red ruby. This is for each day he fasted of the month of Ramadan, besides what he did of good deeds.""**"
"4783","Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people when Ramadan was present. > **""Ramadan has come to you, a month of blessing. Allah enriches you therein, so He sends down mercy, wipes away sins, and answers supplication therein. Allah looks at your competition and boasts of you to His angels. So show Allah good from yourselves. For the wretched is he who is deprived therein of the mercy of Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"4784","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Prophet ﷺ described the adornment of Paradise for Ramadan and the supplication of the Houris. > **""Indeed Paradise is adorned for Ramadan from the beginning of the year to the next year. When the first day of the month of Ramadan comes, a wind blows from beneath the Throne and the leaves of Paradise rustle. The wide-eyed Houris come saying: O Lord, appoint for us from Your servants husbands whose eyes are cooled by us and whose eyes are cooled by them.""**"
"4785","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the reward of one day of Ramadan fasted with hope for reward. > **""Whoever fasts a day of Ramadan seeking reward, for his fasting he shall have that which, if the people of the world gathered since the world began until it ends, would suffice them all in food and drink; he will not ask the people of Paradise for anything of that.""**"
"4786","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the expiation between Ramadans. > **""The fasting of Ramadan to the next Ramadan is expiation for what is between them.""**"
"4787","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the opening of the gates of heaven in Ramadan. > **""Indeed the gates of heaven are opened on the first night of the month of Ramadan and are not closed to the last night of it.""**"
"4788","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announce the arrival of Ramadan and warn the one who is not forgiven in it. > **""This is Ramadan which has come. The gates of Paradise are opened therein, the gates of Fire are closed therein, and the devils are chained therein. Far removed is a person who reaches Ramadan and is not forgiven. If he is not forgiven therein, then when?!""**"
"4789","Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the opening of Paradise, the closing of Fire, the chaining of devils, and the freeing of captives at every fast-breaking. > **""When Ramadan enters, all the gates of Paradise are opened, and no gate of them is closed till the end of the month. The gates of Fire are closed, and no gate of them is opened till the end of the month. The rebellious devils are chained. And Allah has freed ones at every fast-breaking whom He frees from the Fire.""**"
"4790","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeated a glorification three times, Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) asked whether revelation had come or an enemy had arrived, and the Prophet ﷺ explained, then answered a man who recalled the hypocrite. > **""Glory be to Allah, what has come to meet you? And what are you meeting? Indeed Allah forgives on the first night of the month of Ramadan all the people of this qiblah."" And he said to the man who nodded his head: ""It is as though your chest has become constricted?"" And he said: ""The hypocrite is a disbeliever, and there is nothing for a disbeliever in that.""**"
"4791","Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the one who remembers Allah and the one who asks Allah in Ramadan. > **""The one who remembers Allah in Ramadan is forgiven, and the one who asks Allah is not disappointed.""**"
"4792","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised a house in Paradise for a quiet, calm fast. > **""Whoever fasts a day of Ramadan in silence and calm, a house is built for him in Paradise of red ruby or green peridot.""**"
"4793","Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — meaning in Ramadan — promised freed captives and an answered supplication daily. > **""Indeed Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has freed ones in every day and night — meaning in Ramadan — and indeed for every Muslim in every day and night there is an answered supplication.""**"
"4794","Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning every fast-breaking. > **""For Allah there are freed ones at every fast-breaking.""**"
"4795","Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the fasting that expiates what came before it. > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan, knows its limits, and guards therein what he ought to guard, it expiates what came before it.""**"
"4796","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how Paradise and the Houris call for Ramadan, the reward of the one who guards himself, and the warning against sins therein. > **""Indeed Paradise is adorned from year to year for the month of Ramadan. When the month of Ramadan enters, Paradise says: O Allah, appoint for us in this month dwellers from Your servants. And the wide-eyed Houris say: O Allah, appoint for us in this month husbands from Your servants. So whoever guards himself in the month of Ramadan, not drinking therein any intoxicant, not accusing therein a believer of slander, and not committing therein any sin, Allah marries him every night to a hundred Houris and builds for him a palace in Paradise of gold, silver, ruby, and peridot, such that if the world were gathered and placed in that palace, it would be in it only like the tying-place of a goat in this world. And whoever drinks therein an intoxicant, or accuses therein a believer of slander, or commits therein a sin, Allah nullifies his deeds of a year. So beware of the month of Ramadan, for it is the month of Allah, lest you fall short therein. Allah has made for you eleven months in which you enjoy and delight, and He has made for Himself the month of Ramadan. So beware of the month of Ramadan.""**"
"4797","Umm Hani, daughter of Abu Talib (may Allah be pleased with her), reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described when his Ummah is not disgraced, and what disgrace comes from wasting Ramadan. > **""Indeed my Ummah will not be disgraced while they uphold the month of Ramadan."" It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is their disgrace in wasting the month of Ramadan? He said: ""Violating the sacred things therein. Whoever commits fornication therein or drinks therein wine, Allah and those in the heavens curse him till the like of it of the next year. If he dies before he reaches the next Ramadan, he has no good with Allah by which to shield himself from the Fire. So beware of the month of Ramadan. For good deeds are multiplied therein as they are not multiplied in other than it, and likewise evil deeds.""**"
"4798","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) — or al-Hasan — reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning fasting Ramadan with faith and seeking reward. > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan with faith and seeking reward, his past sins and his future sins are forgiven.""**"
"4799","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the excellence of fasting Ramadan in Makkah. > **""Fasting Ramadan in Makkah is better than a thousand Ramadans in other than Makkah.""**"
"4800","Bilal ibn al-Harith (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the excellence of Ramadan and Friday in Madinah. > **""Ramadan in Madinah is better than a thousand Ramadans in other than it, and a Friday in Madinah is better than a thousand Fridays in other lands.""**"
"4801","Talq ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained that Allah made the crescents time-markers for people. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has made these crescents time-markers for people. So fast at its sighting and break your fast at its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then complete the number.""**"
"4802","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded fasting and breaking fast by sighting, and counting thirty when obscured. > **""When you see the crescent, then fast. And when you see it, then break your fast. If it is obscured from you, then count thirty.""**"
"4803","Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded fasting and breaking fast by sighting, completing the number when obscured, and gestured concerning the month. > **""Fast at its sighting and break your fast at its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then complete the number. The month is thus and thus and thus.""**"
"4804","Masruq and al-Bara ibn Azib (may Allah be pleased with him) report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded sighting and counting thirty, and gestured with his hand concerning the month. > **""Fast at its sighting and break your fast at its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then count thirty. The month is thus and thus — meaning twenty-nine.""**"
"4805","Adi ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded fasting Ramadan as thirty unless the crescent is seen earlier. > **""When Ramadan comes, then fast Ramadan as thirty, unless you see the crescent before that.""**"
"4806","Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade anticipating the month and commanded sighting and completing thirty. > **""Do not anticipate — meaning the month of Ramadan — fast at its sighting, and break your fast at its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then complete thirty.""**"
"4807","Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah said he was present in Madinah with Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), when a man came to its governor and testified before him to the sighting of the crescent of Ramadan, and the two Companions ordered its acceptance, explaining the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted the testimony of one man for the sighting of the crescent of Ramadan, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not accept testimony for breaking the fast except the testimony of two men.""**"
"4808","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described among the nearness of the Hour the swelling of crescents. > **""Among the nearness of the Hour is the swelling of the crescents, and that the crescent of one night is seen and it is said: it is of two nights.""**"
"4809","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said that fasting is from the sighting of the crescent to its sighting, and if it is hidden, then thirty days."
"4810","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla said Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to look at the crescent, when a rider appeared. Umar asked from where he had come, he said from Sham. Umar asked whether he had sighted the crescent, he said yes, so Umar said: Allah is Greatest. Then the believers met one another, and the narrator mentioned the rest of the report."
"4811","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the swelling of crescents among the nearness of the Hour. > **""Among the nearness of the Hour is the swelling of the crescents.""**"
"4812","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla reports from al-Bara (may Allah be pleased with him) that he was with Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) when a man came and said he had seen the crescent of Shawwal, so Umar announced to the people to break their fast."
"4813","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that some people testified before the Prophet ﷺ to the sighting of the crescent, so he commanded them to break their fast and to go out in the morning to their Eid."
"4814","Abu Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said the people had begun the morning fasting to complete thirty, when two men came and testified that they had seen the crescent the day before, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the people and they broke their fast. > **""The people broke their fast by the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"4815","Sa'id ibn Amr al-Umawi said it was said to Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that this month had been seen as twenty-nine, and she replied: what amazes you of that? She had fasted with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ twenty-nine more times than they had fasted thirty."
"4816","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) said: do not say the month has decreased, for we fasted with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ twenty-nine more times than we fasted thirty."
"4817","Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said two months do not complete sixty nights, and in another wording sixty days. > **""Two months do not complete sixty nights.""**"
"4818","In another narration from him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning the month and its nights. > **""Indeed the month does not complete thirty nights.""**"
"4819","Some narrators said that not every two months complete thirty — meaning sometimes a month is twenty-nine days."
"4820","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amirah al-Muzani (may Allah be pleased with him) said he memorized five things from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning omens, contagion, the completeness of months, and the protection of Allah. > **""No Safar, no contagion, no Hamah, two months do not complete sixty nights, and whoever violates the protection of Allah will not smell the scent of Paradise.""**"
"4821","Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the sacred months as complete. > **""Every sacred month does not decrease from thirty days and thirty nights.""**"
"4822","Al-Qasim said Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said: two months are fifty-nine days."
"4823","Talq ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he forbade anticipating Ramadan by fasting a day before the sighting or completion, and forbade breaking fast before the sighting or completion. > **""Do not anticipate Ramadan by fasting a day until you see the crescent or the number is fulfilled. Then do not break the fast until you see it or the number is fulfilled.""**"
"4824","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade fasting three days: hastening a day before the sighting, and the day of Fitr, and the day of Adha."
"4825","Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade joining Ramadan with a preceding fast. > **""Do not join Ramadan with a fast.""**"
"4826","Abdullah ibn Abi Musa said Mudrik — or Ibn Mudrik — sent him to Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) to ask her about things, including the day concerning which there is doubt in Ramadan. She said that to fast a day of Sha'ban is dearer to her than to break a day of Ramadan. He then asked Ibn Umar and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them), and each of them said: the wives of the Prophet ﷺ know best about that."
"4827","Masruq said he entered upon Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) on the day doubted to be of Ramadan. She ordered her maidservant to stir sawiq for him. He said he was fasting. She asked whether he had anticipated the month. He said no, but he had fasted all of Sha'ban and that day coincided with it. She said some people used to anticipate the month and fast before the Prophet ﷺ, so Allah revealed: {O you who believe, do not advance before Allah and His Messenger} [al-Hujurat: 1]."
"4828","Muhammad ibn Ka'b said he entered upon Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) at Asr time on a day doubted to be of Ramadan, intending to greet him, when Anas called for food and ate. He asked whether that practice was Sunnah, and Anas replied yes."
"4829","Ibn Ayyash reports from his father that he came to Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) and said some people of his quarter and neighbours had fasted that day. Ibn Mas'ud asked whether it was upon sighting the crescent, he said no, so Ibn Mas'ud replied that to break a day of Ramadan and then make it up is dearer to him than to fast a day of Sha'ban as Ramadan."
"4830","Sufyan ibn Atiyyah ibn Rabi'ah al-Thaqafi (may Allah be pleased with him) said their delegation from Thaqif came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and accepted Islam in the middle of Ramadan, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them, so they fasted and resumed, but he did not command them to make up what had passed them."
"4831","Atiyyah ibn Sufyan reports from Abdullah that the delegation of Thaqif came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Ramadan, and he pitched a tent for them in the mosque, and when they accepted Islam they fasted with him."
"4832","Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would resolve upon fasting from the night, then enter the morning and ask whether there was anything with them, and they would say there was nothing, asking whether he was not fasting."
"4833","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned the one upon whom another Ramadan has come while a previous Ramadan is still due upon him. > **""Whoever is reached by Ramadan while another Ramadan is still due upon him, it will not be accepted from him.""**"
"4834","Uqbah ibn Amir and Fadalah ibn Ubayd (may Allah be pleased with them both) report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would enter the morning in a state of major impurity, then bathe and fast."
"4835","Abdullah ibn Mirdas said a man of his quarter came to him and said he had passed by his wife in the moonlight, she pleased him, so he had intercourse with her in the month of Ramadan, then slept till morning. He advised him to go to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) or Abu Hakim al-Muzani. The man went to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and asked him, and he replied: you were in a state of major impurity in which prayer was not lawful for you, then you bathed, so prayer became lawful for you and fasting became lawful for you, so fast."
"4836","Abdullah ibn Mirdas said he came to the mosque of his quarter after they had prayed Fajr in Ramadan and told them he had been with his wife, then sleep overcame him, so he entered the morning without bathing, asking their view. The people said they viewed him as having broken his fast. He then went to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him, and returning to them he said he had come to one better and more knowledgeable than them, who said that breaking fast is only from food and drink, so complete your fast."
"4837","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said that if he went to his wife at night then deliberately left bathing till morning, that would not prevent him from fasting, for he had approached her when she was lawful to him."
"4838","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that when the month of Ramadan entered, the Prophet ﷺ would free every captive and give to every asker."
"4839","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning taking the pre-dawn meal before fasting. > **""Whoever intends to fast, let him take suhur with something.""**"
"4840","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described suhur as blessing and the mercy upon those who take it. > **""Suhur, its eating is blessing, so do not leave it, even if one of you takes a sip of water. For indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His angels send blessings upon those who take suhur.""**"
"4841","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet ﷺ commanded taking suhur even minimally. > **""Take suhur, even with a sip of water.""**"
"4842","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the blessing upon those who take suhur. > **""Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who take suhur.""**"
"4843","A man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reports that the Prophet ﷺ invoked blessings upon those who take suhur. > **""May Allah bless those who take suhur.""**"
"4844","Al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised dates for suhur and invoked mercy upon those who take suhur. > **""How excellent is suhur with dates. May Allah have mercy upon those who take suhur.""**"
"4845","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reports that the Prophet ﷺ described three who bear no account for what they eat, Allah willing, if it is lawful. > **""Three bear no account for what they eat, Allah willing, if it is lawful: the fasting person, the one who takes suhur, and the one standing guard in the path of Allah.""**"
"4846","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded taking the pre-dawn meal because of the blessing placed in it: > **""Take the sahur, for indeed in the sahur there is blessing.""**"
"4847","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه sent for him and invited him to sahur, informing him of the name the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given it: > **""The blessed morning meal.""**"
"4848","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would call for the blessed meal before dawn, meaning the sahur, even when only two dates were present: > **""Bring us the blessed morning meal.""**"
"4849","Utbah ibn Abd رضي الله عنه and Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded taking sahur in the last part of the night and would describe it as the blessed meal: > **""Take sahur in the last part of the night. It is the blessed morning meal.""**"
"4850","Salman رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed blessing in three matters: > **""Blessing is in three: in the congregation, in tharid, and in the sahur.""**"
"4851","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ praised dates as sahur: > **""How excellent is dates as sahur.""**"
"4852","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that Alqamah ibn Ulathah came to the Prophet ﷺ, who called for a head-meal and began eating with him. Bilal رضي الله عنه came to call to prayer but received no reply, so he returned to the mosque and waited as long as Allah willed, then returned and swore that morning had come. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked mercy for him and explained that the delay would otherwise have been extended toward sunrise. Ali رضي الله عنه added that had Bilal not sworn, the Prophet ﷺ would have continued eating until Jibril عليه السلام told him to lift his hand: > **""May Allah have mercy on Bilal. Had it not been for Bilal, we would have hoped that there would be delay for us between this time and sunrise.""**"
"4853","Alqamah ibn Suhayl al-Thaqafi رضي الله عنه reports that he was among the delegation that came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for whom a tent was pitched near the house of al-Mughirah ibn Shubah رضي الله عنه. Bilal رضي الله عنه would bring them their fast-breaking food while daylight remained, so that they thought it was too early, and when they asked whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had broken his fast, he swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that he had not come until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had broken his fast. Bilal would also bring their sahur while they were seeking warmth, so that when they uncovered the tent-flap their food became visible to them."
"4854","Bilal رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ to announce the prayer while he intended to fast. The Prophet ﷺ called for a cup, drank from it, gave him to drink, then went out to the mosque and stood and prayed without renewing ablution, still intending the fast."
"4855","Bilal رضي الله عنه reports in another wording that he came to announce the prayer to the Prophet ﷺ while he intended to fast, so he drank and then handed the vessel to him before going out to prayer."
"4856","Bilal رضي الله عنه reports in another wording that he came to announce the prayer to the Prophet ﷺ and found him taking sahur at the prayer-place of his house."
"4857","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to see who was in the mosque and invite him. He entered the mosque and found Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه and Umar رضي الله عنه, so he invited them. He then brought some food, placed it before him, and he and they ate; then they went out and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in the morning prayer: > **""See who is in the mosque and invite him.""**"
"4858","Abu al-Zubayr reports that he asked Jabir رضي الله عنه about a man intending the fast with a vessel in his hand from which he is drinking when he hears the call. Jabir said they used to relate that the Prophet ﷺ had ruled concerning it: > **""He should drink.""**"
"4859","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade letting Bilal's adhan prevent sahur, because of weakness in his eyesight: > **""Let not the adhan of Bilal prevent you from the sahur, for indeed there is something in his eyesight.""**"
"4860","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ explained that Bilal calls at night, so eating and drinking should continue until Ibn Umm Maktum calls: > **""Indeed Bilal calls the adhan at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls the adhan.""**"
"4861","Habib ibn Abd al-Rahman reports that he heard his paternal aunt, who had performed Hajj with the Prophet ﷺ, relate that the Prophet ﷺ would say that Ibn Umm Maktum calls at night so they should eat until Bilal calls — or, with doubt, that Bilal calls at night so they should eat until Ibn Umm Maktum calls. She added that one of them would be ascending and the other descending, so they would cling to him and tell him to wait until they had taken sahur: > **""Indeed Ibn Umm Maktum calls at night, so eat and drink until Bilal calls.""**"
"4862","In another wording without doubt, the Prophet ﷺ commanded eating and drinking when Ibn Umm Maktum calls: > **""When Ibn Umm Maktum calls, eat and drink.""**"
"4863","Shayban رضي الله عنه reports that he went early to the mosque and sat by one of the chambers of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ heard his voice, addressed him as Abu Yahya, told him to enter, and when he entered he saw the Prophet ﷺ taking the morning meal and inviting him to it. When he excused himself because he intended to fast, the Prophet ﷺ explained that he too intended to fast, but that their mu'adhdhin had weak eyesight and had called before dawn: > **""Come to the morning meal ... I too intend to fast. Indeed our mu'adhdhin has poor eyesight; he called before dawn.""**"
"4864","Sahl ibn Sad رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command eating and drinking after Bilal's night-call until Ibn Umm Maktum calls, for Ibn Maktum would not call until he was told repeatedly that morning had come: > **""Indeed Bilal calls at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls.""**"
"4865","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took sahur one night while some people were with him. Alqamah ibn Ulathah al-Amiri came, so the Prophet ﷺ invited him to a head-meal. When Bilal رضي الله عنه came to call to prayer, the Prophet ﷺ held him back so that Alqamah could finish his sahur: > **""Gently, O Bilal. Alqamah is taking sahur.""**"
"4866","Amir ibn Matar رضي الله عنه reports that they took sahur with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and then rose for prayer."
"4867","Salman رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Bilal's call should not prevent anyone from his sahur, for he calls only so that the one standing in prayer may return and the sleeper may awake: > **""Let not Bilal prevent any one of you from his sahur, for Bilal only calls so that your standing one in prayer may return, and your sleeper may awake.""**"
"4868","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded eating and drinking after Bilal's night-call until Ibn Umm Maktum calls: > **""Indeed Bilal calls the adhan at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls the adhan.""**"
"4869","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted eating and drinking until Bilal calls: > **""Eat and drink until Bilal calls.""**"
"4870","Habib ibn Abd al-Rahman reports that his paternal aunt, who had performed Hajj with the Prophet ﷺ, related that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded eating and drinking after Bilal's night-call until Ibn Umm Maktum calls. She added that one of them would be ascending and the other descending, so they would cling to him and tell him to wait until they had taken sahur: > **""Indeed Bilal calls at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls the adhan.""**"
"4871","Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه, reports that he was with Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه on a roof in Ramadan while he was praying. He came to him and asked whether he would eat, O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah. He gestured with his hand, and this happened twice. On the third time he said to bring the food, so he ate, prayed two rakahs, then entered the mosque while the prayer was being established."
"4872","Mutayr al-Shaybani reports that they took sahur with Abdullah رضي الله عنه, then went out while the prayer was being established."
"4873","Amr ibn Hurayth رضي الله عنه reports that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were the quickest of people to break the fast and the slowest of them to take sahur."
"4874","Amr ibn Maymun reports that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were the quickest of people to break the fast and the slowest of them to take sahur."
"4875","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked the continued goodness of his Ummah to hastening the fast-breaking and delaying the sahur: > **""My Ummah shall remain in goodness as long as they hasten the iftar and delay the sahur.""**"
"4876","Qutbah ibn Qatadah رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Prophet ﷺ break his fast when the sun had set."
"4877","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid continuous fasting without breaking at night, and used to command hastening the fast-breaking and delaying the sahur."
"4878","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that his Ummah will remain upon his Sunnah as long as they do not delay their fast-breaking until the stars appear: > **""My Ummah shall remain upon my Sunnah as long as they do not wait for the rising of the stars for their fast-breaking.""**"
"4879","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports with the same chain that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fasting, he would command a man to stand upon an elevation of the ground, and when he announced that the sun had set, he would break his fast."
"4880","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the command given to the company of the Prophets to hasten the fast-breaking, delay the sahur, and place the right hands over the left in prayer: > **""Indeed we, the company of the Prophets, have been commanded to hasten our fast-breaking, to delay our sahur, and to place our right hands over our left hands in prayer.""**"
"4881","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ described the three commands given to the company of the Prophets concerning the fast-breaking, the sahur, and the placement of the hands in prayer: > **""Indeed we, the company of the Prophets, have been commanded with three: hastening the fast-breaking, delaying the sahur, and placing the right over the left in prayer.""**"
"4882","Yala ibn Murrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described three matters Allah loves concerning the fast-breaking, the sahur, and the hands in prayer: > **""Three things Allah loves: hastening the iftar, delaying the sahur, and striking one hand over the other in prayer.""**"
"4883","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that he never saw the Prophet ﷺ pray the Maghrib prayer until he had broken his fast, even if only with a sip of water."
"4884","Umm Hakim bint Wada رضي الله عنها reports that she heard the Prophet ﷺ command hastening the fast-breaking and delaying the sahur: > **""Hasten the iftar and delay the sahur.""**"
"4885","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fasting, he would begin the fast-breaking with a drink, and he would not gulp it down but would drink in two or three draughts."
"4886","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ loved to break his fast upon three dates or upon something untouched by fire."
"4887","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fasting, he would not pray until they brought him fresh dates and water, so he would eat and drink; and in winter he would not pray until they brought him dry dates and water."
"4888","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fasting, he would break his fast upon milk. He once came to him with a cup of milk, placed it beside him and covered it while he was praying."
"4889","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey in Ramadan and broke his fast upon Ajwah dates."
"4890","Muhammad ibn Sirin reports that Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما would sometimes break his fast through intercourse."
"4891","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised Allah's nightly emancipation in every night of Ramadan, except for one who breaks his fast upon wine: > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has freed ones in every night of the month of Ramadan, except a man who breaks his fast upon wine.""**"
"4892","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ broke his fast, he would say: > **""In the Name of Allah. O Allah, for You I fasted, and upon Your provision I broke my fast.""**"
"4893","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Prophet ﷺ broke his fast, he would say: > **""For You I fasted, and upon Your provision I broke my fast, so accept from me. Indeed You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.""**"
"4894","Salman رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised the angels' supplication throughout the hours of Ramadan and Jibril's supplication on the Night of Decree for one who gives a fasting person iftar from lawful food and drink, with an addition mentioning tears and soft-heartedness. Salman رضي الله عنه added that even one unable to provide a full meal attains it through a morsel of bread, a taste of milk, or a drink of water: > **""Whoever gives a fasting person iftar upon lawful food and drink, the angels invoke blessings upon him in the hours of the month of Ramadan, and Jibril invokes blessings upon him on the Night of Decree.""**"
"4895","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised one who gives a fasting person iftar a reward like his without any decrease from his reward, and that every righteous deed performed through the strength of that food earns its giver likewise: > **""Whoever gives a fasting person iftar shall have the like of his reward, without anything being diminished from his reward. And no righteous deed performed shall fail to earn its reward for the giver of the food, as long as the strength of the food remains in him.""**"
"4896","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised the like of the fasting person's reward for one who gives him iftar: > **""Whoever gives a fasting person iftar shall have the like of his reward.""**"
"4897","Umm Ishaq رضي الله عنها reports that she was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a bowl of tharid was brought, and she ate with him along with Dhu al-Yadayn رضي الله عنه. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ handed her a bone with meat and invited her to take from it, whereupon she remembered she was fasting and her hand froze. When she explained that she had fasted but forgotten, Dhu al-Yadayn exclaimed at her remembering only after eating her fill, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded her to complete her fast as provision Allah had driven to her: > **""O Umm Ishaq, take from this ... What is the matter with you? ... Complete your fast, for it is only provision Allah drove to you.""**"
"4898","Al-Hasan reports that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded one who eats or drinks forgetfully while fasting to complete his fast, for Allah had fed him and given him drink: > **""When one of you is fasting and forgets, then eats or drinks, let him complete his fast, for indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, fed him and gave him drink.""**"
"4899","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about a fasting person who ate and drank forgetfully, and he did not command him to make it up, explaining it as food Allah had fed him: > **""It is only food Allah fed him.""**"
"4900","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ removed both make-up and expiation from one who eats or drinks forgetfully in Ramadan: > **""Whoever eats or drinks forgetfully in Ramadan, there is no make-up upon him and no expiation.""**"
"4901","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would practise continuous fasting until the pre-dawn."
"4902","Layla, the wife of Bashir رضي الله عنها, reports that she intended to fast two days continuously, but Bashir رضي الله عنه prevented her, explaining that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden it as the practice of the Christians, and had commanded fasting as Allah commanded by completing the fast until night and then breaking it: > **""The Christians do that. But fast as Allah commanded you, and complete the fast until night; then when it is night, break your fast.""**"
"4903","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade them to practise continuous fasting, though not as a strict resolve."
"4904","Abu al-Malih reports from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded joining the fast from one dawn-light to the next: > **""Fast from wadh to wadh.""**"
"4905","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would practise continuous fasting from one sahur to the next sahur."
"4906","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade continuous fasting for three days. When they said that he himself practised it, he explained his distinction through his Lord's feeding and watering: > **""I spend the time with my Lord feeding me and giving me drink.""**"
"4907","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ practised continuous fasting over two days and a night, whereupon Jibril عليه السلام came and told him that Allah had accepted his continuous fast but that it would not be lawful for anyone after him, because Allah says that the fast is completed until night so there is no fasting beyond night, and that he had been commanded to pray Witr after dawn: > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has accepted your continuous fast, and it is not lawful for anyone after you. That is because Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says: {Then complete the fast until night} [al-Baqarah: 187], so there is no fasting after night. And He commanded me to pray Witr after dawn.""**"
"4908","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast in travel and would break his fast, and would pray two rakahs without abandoning them, not adding to them, meaning the obligatory prayer."
"4909","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray barefoot and shod, and fast in travel and break his fast."
"4910","Bishr ibn Harb reports that he asked Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما what he held concerning fasting in travel. He asked whether he would act upon it if informed, and when he agreed, he told him that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left Madinah he would shorten the prayer and would not fast until he returned."
"4911","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would walk barefoot and shod, drink standing and sitting, turn from prayer to the right and to the left, and fast in travel and break his fast."
"4912","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that they set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with some fasting and some breaking their fast, and neither the fasting criticised the breaking nor the breaking criticised the fasting."
"4913","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ with some fasting and some breaking their fast, and neither the fasting criticised the breaking nor the breaking criticised the fasting."
"4914","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast in travel and would break his fast, so he himself fasts and breaks his fast."
"4915","Mithab رضي الله عنه reports that on a campaign with the Prophet ﷺ, every one of them had a mount to take turns upon except him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would dismount and repeatedly invite him to ride, and when he answered that he had strength, he named him Mithab, a name that became most beloved to him. He also travelled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions while some fasted and some broke their fast, without either group criticising the other: > **""Ride ... You are but Mithab.""**"
"4916","Abu al-Ashath al-Attar reports that he asked Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami رضي الله عنه about fasting in travel, and he said they would both fast and break their fast, with neither the breaking criticising the fasting nor the fasting criticising the breaking."
"4917","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that at the campaign of Khaybar the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced a dawn raid and commanded breaking the fast to gain strength: > **""We shall raid them at dawn, so break your fast and gain strength.""**"
"4918","Utbah ibn Abd al-Sulami رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how an obligatory day's fast and a voluntary day's fast in Allah's path distance Hell as far as between the heavens and the earth: > **""Whoever fasts a day as an obligation in the path of Allah, Allah distances Hell from him as between the heavens and the earth. And whoever fasts a day voluntarily, Allah distances Hell from him the distance of what is between heaven and earth.""**"
"4919","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that they set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Ramadan, so he fasted and his Companions fasted with him; then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ broke his fast and his Companions broke theirs with him, while the fasting one had been superior to the breaking one."
"4920","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey in Ramadan when a vessel was brought and placed upon his hand. When the people saw him, they broke their fast."
"4921","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out for the expedition of Hunayn when eighteen nights of Ramadan had passed, while he was fasting. The troops passed by a river and fixed their gaze upon it. > **""Will you drink?""** They said, shall we drink while you are fasting. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for a vessel and drank. When he finished Hunayn and Taif, he came to al-Jiirranah, divided the spoils there, and entered Umrah from it."
"4922","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out when fourteen nights of Ramadan had passed. He made his mount kneel, placed one foot in the stirrup and the other upon the ground, then called for milk from its milk and drank it."
"4923","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ travelled in Ramadan and fasted one day until the afternoon, then broke his fast, then fasted again and completed the fast until the night."
"4924","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ travelled in Ramadan, and fasting bore heavily upon a man among his Companions, so his she-camel wandered with him beneath the shade of trees. The Prophet ﷺ was informed and ordered him, so he broke his fast. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for a vessel containing water, placed it upon his hand, and when the people saw him drink, they drank."
"4925","Abu Barzah al-Aslami (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fasting in travel is not part of righteousness.""**"
"4926","Kab ibn Asim al-Ashari (رضي الله عنه), who was of the people of al-Saqifah, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say in the dialect of Himyar: > **""Laysa min ambrr imsiyamu fi amsafar.""** meaning fasting in travel is not part of righteousness."
"4927","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Righteousness is not fasting in travel.""**"
"4928","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ travelled and halted with his Companions, when some people had made a shelter of branches over their companion while he was fasting. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed them and asked about his condition and whether he was ill. They said, no, O Messenger of Allah, but he is fasting, and that was on a scorching day. > **""It is no righteousness to fast in travel.""**"
"4929","Ammar ibn Yasir (رضي الله عنه) reports that they advanced with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from an expedition, journeying on a day of severe heat, and halted along the road. A man went and entered beneath a tree while his companions sheltered him, lying as one in pain. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw them, he asked about his condition. They said, he is fasting. > **""It is not part of righteousness that you fast in travel. Hold to the concession which Allah has granted you, and accept it.""**"
"4930","Umm al-Darda (رضي الله عنها), through Abu al-Darda (رضي الله عنه), reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fasting in travel is not part of righteousness.""**"
"4931","Muawiyah (رضي الله عنه) said that fasting in travel is not from the Sunnah."
"4932","Zurarah ibn Awfa reports from a man among his people that he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ while he was eating, and he invited him forward. The man said, I am fasting. > **""Come forward, I shall tell you. Allah the Exalted has lifted fasting and half the prayer from the traveler.""**"
"4933","Abu al-Fayd reports that Maslamah ibn Abd al-Malik addressed them saying, do not fast Ramadan in travel, and whoever fasts must make it up. Abu al-Fayd then met Abu Qirsafah Wathilah ibn al-Asqa (رضي الله عنه) and asked him, and he replied that if one fasted, then one has fasted and need not make it up."
"4934","Uthman ibn Abi al-As (رضي الله عنه) said that breaking fast in travel is a concession."
"4935","Uthman ibn Abi al-As (رضي الله عنه) used to prefer fasting in travel, saying it was a concession."
"4936","Abu Tumah reports that he was with Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) when a man came and said, O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I am strong enough to fast in travel. Ibn Umar replied that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever does not accept the concession of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, upon him is sin like the mountains of Arafah.""**"
"4937","Uqbah ibn Amir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever does not accept the concession of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, upon him is sins like the mountains of Arafah.""**"
"4938","Amr ibn Hazm (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever does not accept the concession of Allah, upon him is sin like the mountains of Uhud.""**"
"4939","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, loves that His concessions be acted upon as He hates that His disobedience be committed.""**"
"4940","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, loves that His concessions be acted upon as He loves that His binding commands be acted upon.""**"
"4941","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves that His concessions be accepted as He loves that His binding commands be fulfilled.""**"
"4942","Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Adam reports that Abu al-Darda, Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, Abu Umamah, and Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنهم) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah loves that His concessions be accepted as the servant loves His forgiveness.""**"
"4943","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah loves that His concessions be taken as He loves that His binding commands be taken.""** When asked what His binding commands are, he said: > **""His prescribed duties.""**"
"4944","Muadh ibn Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever is fasting, visits the sick, and attends a funeral shall be forgiven, unless he commits a new deed thereafter.""**"
"4945","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions one day: > **""Who among you attended a funeral today? Who among you visited the sick? Who gave charity? Who woke fasting?""** To each, Umar (رضي الله عنه) replied, I did. Then he said: > **""It has become binding, it has become binding.""**"
"4946","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Prophet ﷺ asked his Companions which of them woke fasting, visited the sick, followed a funeral, and fed the poor. To each, Abu Bakr (رضي الله عنه) replied, I did, O Messenger of Allah. > **""Whoever has these four, a house shall be built for him in Paradise.""**"
"4947","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether any had woken fasting, visited the sick, or given charity that day. The people fell silent while Abu Bakr (رضي الله عنه) replied to each, I did, O Messenger of Allah. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed profusely and said: > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, none gathers them in a single day except a believer, and he shall enter Paradise thereby.""**"
"4948","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked who woke fasting, who visited the sick, and who followed a funeral. To each, Abu Bakr (رضي الله عنه) replied, I did. > **""Whoever gathers them in a single day shall enter Paradise.""**"
"4949","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the dawn prayer, turned to his Companions, and asked whether any woke fasting, visited the sick, or fed a poor person that day. Umar (رضي الله عنه) replied that he had not intended a fast and had only just prayed, so how could he have visited the sick. Abu Bakr (رضي الله عنه) replied that he had intended the fast, had passed by his brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf who was ill on his way to the mosque, and had taken a piece of barley bread from the hand of Abd al-Rahman and given it to a beggar in the mosque. > **""You, then glad tidings of Paradise. May Allah have mercy upon Umar, may Allah have mercy upon Umar. He never intends any good but Abu Bakr precedes him to it.""**"
"4950","Qatadah reports that Anas (رضي الله عنه) grew too weak to fast a year before his death, so he broke his fast and fed a poor person for each day."
"4951","Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah reports that Anas (رضي الله عنه) grew weak for fasting, so he prepared a large bowl of tharid and invited thirty poor persons and fed them."
"4952","Mujahid reports that Qays ibn al-Saib grew old until sixty years passed him beyond a hundred and grew too weak to fast, so food was given on his behalf."
"4953","In another narration, Qays ibn al-Saib (رضي الله عنه) said that for the month of Ramadan a man may ransom himself by feeding a poor person for each day, so feed on my behalf a sa for a poor person for each day. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was his partner in the Jahiliyyah, and the best partner who neither disputed nor quarrelled in bargaining."
"4954","Ali and Khabbab (رضي الله عنهما) report from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""When you fast, use the siwak in the morning and do not use it in the evening. For no fasting person whose lips dry in the evening but it shall be a light before his eyes on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4955","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm reports that he asked Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) whether he may use the siwak while fasting, and at which part of the day. He replied, at whichever part you wish, morning or evening. When told that people dislike it in the evening because the breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah than musk, Muadh glorified Allah and replied that the Prophet ﷺ commanded the siwak knowing the mouth of the fasting person must have some odor even if he uses it, and he did not command them deliberately to make their mouths foul, for there is no good in that but evil, except for one afflicted with an unavoidable trial. Likewise the dust in the path of Allah is rewarded only for one compelled to it with no escape. > **""The breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk.""**"
"4956","Ibn Abasah (رضي الله عنه) reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rinse his mouth and sniff water into his nose in Ramadan."
"4957","Abdullah ibn Thalabah ibn Suayr (رضي الله عنه), whose face the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had wiped and who met the Companions, reports that they used to forbid him from kissing while fasting fearing he would proceed to more than it, and that Muslims today forbid him, one of them saying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had Allah's protection which none else has."
"4958","Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) reports that he saw the Prophet ﷺ in sleep and he would not look at him. When asked what was the matter, he replied: > **""Are you not the one who kisses while fasting?""** Umar said, by Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Umar, I shall never kiss while fasting again."
"4959","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade a man to kiss while fasting."
"4960","Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) used to forbid the fasting person from kissing, saying none of you has the immunity which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had."
"4961","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) said concerning a man who kisses while fasting that he should make up a day in its place. Sufyan said it is not acted upon."
"4962","Abdullah ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when a youth came and asked whether he may kiss while fasting, and he said no. Then an elder came and asked whether he may kiss while fasting, and he said yes. When they looked at one another, the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I know why you looked at one another. The elder controls himself.""**"
"4963","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that when a youth asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about kissing, he forbade him, and when an elder asked, he permitted him, saying: > **""The youth is not like the elder.""**"
"4964","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) permitted the elder to kiss while fasting and forbade the youth."
"4965","Atiyyah reports that a youth asked Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) whether he may kiss while fasting and he said no, then an elder asked and he said yes. The youth objected how that could be lawful for him and forbidden for himself when both are upon one religion. Ibn Abbas replied that a vein of the testicles is attached to the nose, so when the nose smells the organ is stirred, and when stirred it calls to what is greater than that, while the elder controls his desire. This was after the sight of Abdullah had gone, and a woman behind him remarked, may Allah disgrace you as a companion of a people."
"4966","Ata ibn Yasar reports from a man of the Ansar (رضي الله عنه) that he kissed his wife while fasting in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he ordered his wife to ask the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said that the Messenger of Allah does that. When his wife informed him, he said the Prophet is granted concessions in some matters, so return and tell him. When she returned and said so, he replied: > **""I am the most Godfearing of you toward Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the most knowing of you of His limits.""**"
"4967","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to take from the heads while fasting, meaning he would kiss the head."
"4968","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked whether the fasting person may kiss, and he replied: > **""What harm is in that, a sweet basil he smells?""**"
"4969","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to kiss while fasting."
"4970","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and asked for a morsel. She brought a round loaf, he placed it upon his mouth, then said: > **""O Aishah, did anything of it enter my belly. So is the kiss of the fasting person. Breaking fast is only by what enters, not by what exits.""**"
"4971","Abu Rafi (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to apply ithmid kohl while fasting."
"4972","Barirah (رضي الله عنها), the freedwoman of Aishah, reports that she saw the Prophet ﷺ apply ithmid kohl while fasting."
"4973","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined him: > **""Wake on your fast day oiled and combed, and do not wake on your fast day frowning.""**"
"4974","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) said to wake oiled while fasting."
"4975","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said he had broken fast one day of Ramadan. He asked whether it was without excuse or travel, and the man said yes. He told him what an evil deed he had done, and when asked what he commands, he ordered him to free a slave, fast two consecutive months, and feed sixty poor persons, while the man declared inability for each. Then the Prophet ﷺ was brought a measure containing dates and ordered him to give it to the poorest he knows. When the man swore none between its two lava tracts was needier than his household, he said: > **""Without excuse or travel? What an evil deed you have done. Free a slave. Then fast two consecutive months. Then feed sixty poor. Give this in charity to sixty poor. To the poorest you know. Then give it in charity to your dependents.""**"
"4976","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه) reports that a man said, O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined, I broke fast in the month of Ramadan deliberately. > **""Free a slave. Fast two consecutive months. Feed sixty poor.""**"
"4977","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said he had deliberately broken fast one day of Ramadan and had intercourse with his wife therein. He ordered him to free a slave, offer a sacrificial camel, and give twenty or nineteen or twenty-one sa of dates, while the man declared inability for each. Then the Prophet ﷺ was brought a measure containing twenty sa of dates and ordered him to give it in charity. When the man said no household in Madinah was needier than his, he said: > **""Free a slave. Offer a sacrificial camel. Give twenty sa of dates in charity. Give this in charity. Then feed your household with it.""**"
"4978","Ata and Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, report the like from the Prophet ﷺ, with the addition of a sacrificial camel, and Amr added in his narration that he ordered him to fast a day in its place. > **""Eat it you and your dependents.""**"
"4979","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) said that whoever breaks fast one day of Ramadan without concession shall meet Allah with it, even if he fasts the whole year, and if He wills He forgives him and if He wills He punishes him."
"4980","Bilal (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4981","Usamah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The cupper and the one seeking cupping have broken fast.""**"
"4982","Maqil ibn Sinan al-Ashjai (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed him while he was being cupped when eighteen nights of Ramadan had passed, and said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4983","Maqil ibn Yasar (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed him while he was being cupped when eighteen nights of Ramadan had passed, and said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4984","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) and Aishah (رضي الله عنها) report from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""The cupper and the one seeking cupping have broken fast.""**"
"4985","Ali (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4986","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4987","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4988","Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4989","Abu Rafi (رضي الله عنه) reports that he entered upon Abu Musa (رضي الله عنه) while he was being cupped at night, and suggested daylight. Abu Musa replied, would you order me to shed my blood while fasting, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4990","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4991","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast.""**"
"4992","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ ordered Abu Taybah to set the cupping instruments at sunset, then ordered him along with the breaking of the fasting person's fast, so he cupped him. He then asked him about his fee, and when he said two sa, the Prophet ﷺ reduced a sa for him."
"4993","Abu Said (رضي الله عنه) said that cupping was disliked for the fasting person only on account of weakness."
"4994","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped while fasting and in ihram, then fainted, and for that reason cupping was disliked for the fasting person."
"4995","Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while fasting."
"4996","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that Abu Taybah passed by them in Ramadan, the narrator thinks after Asr, and mentioned that he had cupped the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"4997","It is reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for a cupper known as Abu Taybah, who then cupped him after Asr in Ramadan."
"4998","Abu Said رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ granted a concession for cupping for the fasting person, and in one wording for kissing and cupping for the fasting person."
"4999","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped in Ramadan."
"5000","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that Abu Taybah passed by them in the month of Ramadan, and when asked where he had come from, he mentioned that he had cupped the Prophet ﷺ."
"5001","Abdullah ibn Sufyan رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while he was fasting."
"5002","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while fasting and gave the cupper his wage, for had it been unlawful he would not have given it to him."
"5003","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped after having made the statement about the cupper and the one cupped breaking their fast. > **""The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast.""**"
"5004","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three things do not break the fast of the fasting person: vomiting, cupping, and a wet dream.""**"
"5005","Thawban رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three things do not hinder the fasting person: cupping, vomiting, and a wet dream, but the fasting person should not deliberately induce vomit.""**"
"5006","On the authority of Thawban رضي الله عنه it is reported: > **""Three things do not break the fast of the fasting person.""** And the narrator then mentioned the same as above."
"5007","Abdullah al-Sunabihi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever enters the morning fasting, then has a wet dream, or is cupped, or is overcome by vomit, then there is no makeup upon him, but whoever deliberately vomits, then makeup is upon him.""**"
"5008","Ubayd, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that two women were fasting, and a man said, O Messenger of Allah, two women here have fasted and have nearly died of thirst. He turned away from him or remained silent. He repeated it, during midday heat, swearing by Allah that they had died or nearly died. He said: > **""Call them both.""** They came, and a cup or large vessel was brought. He said to one of them: > **""Vomit.""** She vomited pus, blood, and festering flesh until she filled half the vessel. He then said to the other: > **""Vomit.""** She vomited pus, blood, festering flesh, and raw meat until she filled the vessel. He then said: > **""These two fasted from what Allah made lawful for them, and broke their fast upon what Allah made unlawful for them. One of them sat with the other, and they began eating the flesh of the people.""**"
"5009","In one narration, the people had been commanded to fast, and a man came during part of the day and said, O Messenger of Allah, such-and-such woman and such-and-such woman have reached extreme hardship."
"5010","In one narration, Sad, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that the people had been commanded to fast, with a similar account containing an unnamed man in its chain."
"5011","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever does not abandon foul speech and lying, then Allah has no need for him to abandon his food and drink.""**"
"5012","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fasting is a shield, so long as he does not tear it.""** When asked how he tears it, he said: > **""By lying or backbiting.""**"
"5013","Al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts a day and does not tear it, ten good deeds are written for him.""**"
"5014","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the sky rained hail, and Abu Talhah رضي الله عنه, while they were young boys, asked him to hand him some of the hail. He did so, and he began eating it while fasting. When asked whether he was fasting, he replied that it was neither food nor drink, but a blessing from the sky by which they purify their stomachs. Anas then informed the Prophet ﷺ, who said: > **""Take from your uncle.""**"
"5015","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever stands in Ramadan out of faith and hope for reward, his previous sins are forgiven.""**"
"5016","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ encouraging standing in Ramadan, though the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not gather the people together for the standing."
"5017","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that he asked to spend the night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and pray with his prayer. He said: > **""You cannot bear it.""** The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then rose to bathe behind a cloth while he turned away, then he did the same. They stood in prayer together until, from the length of his prayer, he began striking his head against the walls. Bilal رضي الله عنه then came to call for prayer, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had done so. When he replied yes, he said: > **""O Bilal, you call the adhan when the morning is shining lengthwise in the sky, but that is not the true dawn. The true dawn is like this, spreading across.""** He then called for his suhur and took the pre-dawn meal."
"5018","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ would pray twenty rakahs and witr in Ramadan."
"5019","Zayd ibn Wahb reports that Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه would lead them in prayer in the month of Ramadan, and they would leave while it was still night."
"5020","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in eight rakahs and witr in the month of Ramadan. The next night they gathered in the mosque hoping he would come out to them, but he remained inside until morning. When asked about it, he said: > **""I feared — or disliked — that it would be made obligatory upon you.""**"
"5021","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would pray at night in Ramadan, and some people came and prayed. He prayed briefly, then entered his house and continued praying, then came out and prayed briefly. In the morning they mentioned standing behind him and his going into his house and coming out. He said: > **""I only did that on your account.""**"
"5022","Abu Layla رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ observing itikaf in a dome made of palm fronds."
"5023","Muayqib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed itikaf in a dome of palm fronds whose door was of matting, while the people were in the mosque."
"5024","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ observed itikaf in the first ten days the first year, then in the middle ten, then in the last ten, and said: > **""I was shown Laylat al-Qadr during it, then it was made forgotten to me.""** He then continued to observe itikaf in the last ten until he passed away ﷺ."
"5025","Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Itikaf for ten days in Ramadan is like two Hajjs and two Umrahs.""**"
"5026","Abu Wail reports that Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه said to Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه about people observing itikaf between his house and the house of Abu Musa, asking why he did not forbid them. Abdullah replied that perhaps they were right and he was wrong, and they remembered while he forgot. Hudhayfah maintained that he knew itikaf is only in these three mosques: the mosque of Madinah, the mosque of Makkah, and the mosque of Iliya."
"5027","In one narration, Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه said that he knew itikaf is only in a mosque where congregation is held."
"5028","Ibrahim reports that Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه came to Abdullah رضي الله عنه and expressed astonishment at people observing itikaf between his house and the house of al-Ashari. Abdullah replied that perhaps they were right and he was wrong. Hudhayfah said he would not care whether he observed itikaf there or in their houses, for itikaf is only in these three mosques: al-Masjid al-Haram, the mosque of Madinah, and al-Masjid al-Aqsa. Those whom Hudhayfah criticized had observed itikaf in the grand mosque of Kufa."
"5029","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when the last ten days entered, the Prophet ﷺ would fold his bedding, withdraw from women, and make his evening meal his suhur."
"5030","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would wake his family in the last ten days of Ramadan, every young and old who was able to pray."
"5031","Ali رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten days, and if you are overcome, then do not be overcome regarding the remaining seven.""**"
"5032","Ali رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""I saw the moon on Laylat al-Qadr as though it were half a bowl.""**"
"5033","Ali رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I came out when the moon had risen as though it were a split bowl.""** And he said: > **""Tonight is Laylat al-Qadr.""**"
"5034","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seek it in the last ten, on the odd nights.""**"
"5035","Abu Aqrab reports that he came to Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه one morning in Ramadan and found him sitting above his house, saying, Allah has spoken the truth and His Messenger has conveyed. When asked about it, he said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Laylat al-Qadr is in the middle of the last seven of Ramadan. The sun rises on its morning clear, having no rays.""** He looked at it and found it just as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said."
"5036","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and he asked who among them remembered the night of al-Sahbawat. Abdullah said, may my father and mother be ransomed for you, I do, with dates in my hand with which I was taking suhur, screened by the back of my saddle from the dawn, when the moon was rising. In one addition, that was the night of the twenty-seventh."
"5037","Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten.""** With the addition: > **""In the last ten of Ramadan, on the odd nights, for I had seen it then forgot it, and it is a night of drizzle and wind.""** And in one wording, rain and wind, with the addition of thunder."
"5038","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about Laylat al-Qadr and said: > **""It is in the last ten. Stand on the third or the fifth remaining.""**"
"5039","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports regarding the commander of the expedition, Ghalib al-Laythi, and Qutbah ibn Amir who entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the palms while he was in ihram, and Abdullah ibn Unays who asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Laylat al-Qadr after twenty-two nights had passed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seek it in these seven last nights remaining from the month.""**"
"5040","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Laylat al-Qadr, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""In Ramadan, so seek it in the last ten, for it is on an odd night: the twenty-first, or twenty-third, or twenty-fifth, or twenty-seventh, or twenty-ninth, or the last night. Whoever stands it seeking it out of faith and hope for reward, then is granted it, his past and future sins are forgiven.""**"
"5041","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Laylat al-Qadr is in the remaining ten. Whoever stands them seeking their reward, then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, forgives his past and future sins. It is an odd night: the ninth, seventh, fifth, third, or last night.""** And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed the sign of Laylat al-Qadr is that it is clear and bright, as though a shining moon is in it, calm and mild, with neither cold nor heat in it, and no star is cast in it until morning. And its sign is that the sun on its morning rises level, having no rays like the full moon, and Satan is not permitted to come out with it that day.""**"
"5042","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning Laylat al-Qadr: > **""It is the twenty-seventh or twenty-ninth night. Indeed the angels that night on earth are more numerous than pebbles.""**"
"5043","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Seek Laylat al-Qadr on the seventeenth, nineteenth, twenty-first, twenty-third, twenty-fifth, twenty-seventh, or twenty-ninth.""**"
"5044","Bilal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Laylat al-Qadr is the night of the twenty-fourth.""**"
"5045","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning Laylat al-Qadr: > **""Whoever seeks it, let him seek it on the twenty-seventh night.""** And he said: > **""Seek it on the twenty-seventh night.""**"
"5046","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that while sleeping in Ramadan he was told in a dream that that night was Laylat al-Qadr. He rose while drowsy, clung to one of the ropes of the tent of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and came to him while he was praying. He examined that night, and it was the twenty-third night."
"5047","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Prophet of Allah, I am an elderly, ailing old man, so command me with one night in which perhaps Allah will grant me Laylat al-Qadr. He said: > **""Hold to the seventh.""**"
"5048","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that al-Juhani said, O Messenger of Allah, we are where you know, and we cannot attend this whole month, so inform us of Laylat al-Qadr. He said: > **""Attend the last seven.""** When he said he could not do that, he said: > **""Seek it on the night with seven remaining, and it is this night.""** The narrator said, I said, O Messenger of Allah, this is the twenty-third night, with eight remaining? He said: > **""Thus this month decreases, and they are seven remaining.""**"
"5049","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ came out one night intending to inform them of Laylat al-Qadr, of which he had been informed, but he heard clamour in the mosque, so it was taken away from him."
"5050","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about Laylat al-Qadr and said: > **""I had been informed of it, then it slipped from me, so seek it in the seven remaining or three remaining.""**"
"5051","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Seek it in the last ten, on the ninth, seventh, and fifth.""**"
"5052","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Laylat al-Qadr is a bright night, neither hot nor cold.""**"
"5053","Marthad reports that he met Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه at the middle jamrah and asked him about Laylat al-Qadr. He said no one asked about it more than he did. He said, I said, O Messenger of Allah, was it sent down upon the prophets by revelation to them then lifted? He said: > **""Rather it remains until the Day of Resurrection.""** I said, O Messenger of Allah, which of them is it? He said: > **""If I were permitted I would inform you of it, but seek it in the ninth and seventh, and do not ask me after it.""** He then said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned and began speaking, so he asked in which of the seven it is. He became angrier with him than ever before or after, then said: > **""Did I not forbid you from it? If I were permitted I would inform you of it, but it may be in the last seven.""**"
"5054","Uqbah ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and addressed the people upon the pulpit in Ramadan and said: > **""I stood upon this pulpit while I knew Laylat al-Qadr, so seek it in the last ten, on the odd nights.""**"
"5055","Kab ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and addressed the people upon the pulpit in Ramadan and said: > **""I stood upon the pulpit while I knew Laylat al-Qadr, so seek it in the last ten, on the odd.""**"
"5056","Kab ibn Ujrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the pulpit and said: > **""I ascended while I knew, and I had known Laylat al-Qadr, so seek it in the last ten, on the odd.""**"
"5057","Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seek Laylat al-Qadr on the night of the twenty-seventh.""**"
"5058","Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit reports from his father that he would enliven the twenty-third night of Ramadan and the twenty-seventh night, but not like his enlivening of the seventeenth night. When asked how he enlivened the seventeenth, he said the Quran descended in it, and on its morning truth was distinguished from falsehood at Badr, and in it he would wake bright-faced."
"5059","Hawt al-Abdi reports that he asked Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said he had no doubt or uncertainty that it is the seventeenth night, the night the Quran was sent down and the day the two hosts met."
"5060","Al-Faltan ibn Asim رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while they sat waiting for him, and he came out with anger on his face. He sat long without speaking, then it was lifted from him, and he said: > **""I came out to you while Laylat al-Qadr and the false messiah had become clear to me, and I came out to clarify them for you and give you glad tidings of them. Then I met in the mosque two men quarreling with Satan between them, so I stopped between them, and it was taken from me. It is in the last ten. As for the false messiah, he is bald in the forehead, wiped in the eye, broad in the chest, with the likeness of Ibn al-Uzza in him.""** In one narration: > **""As for Laylat al-Qadr, seek it in the last ten.""**"
"5061","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came quickly while they sat, and his haste alarmed them. When he reached them he greeted them, then said: > **""I came to you to inform you of Laylat al-Qadr, then I forgot it between me and you.""** Then he mentioned the hadith."
"5062","Abdullah ibn Unays رضي الله عنه reports that he said, O Messenger of Allah, inform me in which night Laylat al-Qadr is sought. He said: > **""Were it not that the people would abandon prayer except that night, I would have informed you.""**"
"5063","Abdullah ibn Jahsh reports from his father that he said, O Messenger of Allah, I have a desert dwelling where I pray, so command me with a night I may descend to the mosque and pray in it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Descend on the night of the twenty-third.""**"
"5064","Awf ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in itikaf in the last ten of Ramadan, and when the twenty-third night came, he said: > **""Whoever loves to stand with us this night, let him stand.""** He led them until a third of the night had passed, then left. He walked with him until he reached his dome, and they said, O Messenger of Allah, if only you had led us this whole night. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It suffices a man to stand with the imam until he leaves; a night's standing is reckoned for him.""**"
"5065","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""Laylat al-Qadr is a bright night, neither hot nor cold, with no clouds, rain, or wind in it, and no star is cast in it. And from the signs of its day, the sun rises having no rays.""**"
"5066","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""Whoever reaches Ramadan while owing something from a past Ramadan which he has not made up, it is not accepted from him, and whoever fasts voluntarily while owing something from Ramadan which he has not made up, it is not accepted from him until he fasts it.""**"
"5067","Umar رضي الله عنه reports that when anything of Ramadan was missed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he would make it up in the ten of Dhul-Hijjah."
"5068","In the narration of al-Awsat, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw no harm in making up Ramadan in the ten of Dhul-Hijjah."
"5069","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever dies owing fasts, then his guardian should fast on his behalf, if he wishes.""**"
"5070","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fight and you will gain spoils, fast and you will be healthy, travel and you will become independent.""**"
"5071","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports: I heard Abu al-Qasim ﷺ say: > **""Allah Almighty says: Every deed is expiation except fasting, and fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it.""**"
"5072","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah Almighty has made the good deed of the son of Adam tenfold up to seven hundredfold except fasting, for fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it. And the fasting person has two joys: a joy at his breaking of fast and a joy on the Day of Resurrection. And the breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk.""** In an addition from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""When it is the day of fast of one of you, let him not utter foul speech nor act ignorantly, and if an ignorant person acts ignorantly against him, let him say: I am fasting.""**"
"5073","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""In Paradise is a gate called Rayyan; none shall enter through it on the Day of Resurrection except those who fast.""**"
"5074","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no servant who enters the morning fasting except that the doors of heaven are opened for him, his limbs glorify for him, and the inhabitants of the lowest heaven seek forgiveness for him until he is veiled behind the veil. If he prays one or two voluntary rakahs, the heavens are lit with light for him, and his wives from the black-eyed maidens say: O Allah, take him to us, for we long to see him. If he utters tahlil or tasbih or takbir, angels receive it and record it until he is veiled behind the veil.""**"
"5075","Abu Said رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""The breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk. He said: This one fasted for My sake and abandoned the desire for food for My sake, so fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it.""**"
"5076","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Fasting is a shield and a fortified fortress from the Fire.""**"
"5077","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ: > **""Allah said: Fasting is a shield by which the servant shields himself from the Fire; it is for Me and I shall reward for it.""**"
"5078","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fasting is a shield, and it is a fortress among the fortresses of the believer, and every deed is for its doer, and fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it.""**"
"5079","Wathilah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fasting is a shield, and it is a fortress among the fortresses of the believer, and every deed is for its doer except fasting. Allah Almighty says: Fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it.""**"
"5080","Qatadah reports from Jurayy ibn Kulayb, from Bashir ibn al-Khasasiyah رضي الله عنه, and our companions narrated from Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ, narrating from his Lord Most High, said: > **""Fasting is a shield by which My servant shields himself from the Fire, and fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it; he leaves his food and his desire for My sake. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, the breath of the fasting person on the Day of Resurrection is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk.""**"
"5081","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fasting and the Quran shall intercede for the servant on the Day of Resurrection. Fasting shall say: O Lord, I prevented him from food and desire, so accept my intercession for him. And the Quran shall say: I prevented him from sleep at night, so accept my intercession for him. He said: So they shall intercede for him.""**"
"5082","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I shall inform you of doors of goodness: fasting is a shield, and charity extinguishes sin as water extinguishes fire, and the standing of the servant in the depth of the night.""** He then recited: > **""Their sides forsake their beds.""**"
"5083","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that he said: O Messenger of Allah, what is fasting? He said: > **""An obligation that suffices.""**"
"5084","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to castrate myself? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The castration of my Ummah is fasting and standing.""**"
"5085","Salamah ibn Qaysar رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts a day seeking the Face of Allah, Allah shall distance him from Hell like the distance of a crow that flew as a chick until it died old.""**"
"5086","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts a day seeking the Face of Allah, Allah shall distance him from Hell like the distance of a crow that flew as a chick until it died old.""**"
"5087","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prepared an expedition, so I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate for me for martyrdom. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils.""** He said: So we were kept safe and gained spoils. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prepared a second campaign, so I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate for me for martyrdom. He said: > **""O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils.""** He said: So we were kept safe and gained spoils. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prepared a third campaign, so I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I came to you twice before this time of mine and asked you to supplicate Allah for me for martyrdom, and you said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils, so we were kept safe and gained spoils. O Messenger of Allah, command me to a deed. He said: > **""Hold fast to fasting, for there is nothing like it.""** He said: So Abu Umamah and neither his wife nor his servant were ever seen except fasting. He said: When smoke was seen in their house during the day, it would be said: A guest has come to them, a visitor has alighted upon them. He said: I remained thus for as long as Allah willed, then I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, you commanded us to fast, and I hope Allah has blessed us therein. O Messenger of Allah, command me to another deed? He said: > **""Know that you shall never prostrate to Allah with a prostration except that Allah raises you a degree thereby and removes a sin from you thereby.""**"
"5088","Sahl ibn Sad رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Everything has a zakat, and the zakat of the body is fasting.""**"
"5089","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said: I do not grieve over anything that I missed except fasting and prayer, and my abandoning the rebellious party unless I had fought them, and my withdrawing the pledge from Ali. In one wording: I do not grieve over anything I missed of this world except fasting in the midday heats, and that I did not part my feet in prayer, meaning lengthening the prayer."
"5090","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Deeds are seven: two deeds that save, two deeds requited with their like, a deed requited tenfold, a deed requited seven hundredfold, and a deed whose reward its doer knows not except Allah. As for the two that save: whoever meets Allah Almighty worshipping Him sincerely, associating nothing with Him, Paradise becomes due for him; and whoever meets Allah associating anything with Him, the Fire becomes due for him. Whoever does an evil deed shall be requited thereby, and whoever intends to do a good deed but does not do it shall be requited with its like, and whoever does a good deed shall be requited tenfold, and whoever spends his wealth in the path of Allah, his spending of a dirham is multiplied seven hundredfold, and fasting, none knows the reward of its doer except Allah Almighty.""**"
"5091","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Fasting emaciates the flesh and distances from the heat of Hell. Allah has a table upon which is what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and has never occurred to the heart of man; none shall sit at it except those who fast.""**"
"5092","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If a man were to fast a voluntary day then be given the earth full of gold, he would not fully receive its reward before the Day of Reckoning.""**"
"5093","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah Blessed and Most High said: Fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it. And by the oath of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: The breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk, so whichever of you enters the morning fasting, let him not utter foul speech nor act ignorantly, and if a man fights him, let him say: I am fasting, for they shall have on the Day of Resurrection a basin to which none shall come except those who fast.""**"
"5094","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Whoever is sealed with fasting shall enter Paradise.""**"
"5095","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Abu Musa رضي الله عنه on an expedition by sea, and while they were thus, when they raised the sails on a dark night, a caller called from above them: O people of the ship, stop, I shall inform you of a decree Allah decreed upon Himself. Abu Musa said: Inform us if you are to inform?! He said: > **""Allah Blessed and Most High decreed upon Himself that whoever makes himself thirsty for Him on a summer day, Allah shall give him drink on the Day of Thirst.""**"
"5096","Qays ibn Yazid al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts a voluntary day, a tree shall be planted for him in Paradise, its fruit smaller than the pomegranate and larger than the apple, its sweetness like the sweetness of honeycomb and its delight like the delight of honey; Allah shall feed the fasting person therefrom on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"5097","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports: Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: How did you wake, O Messenger of Allah? He said: > **""Sound, well, better than a man who did not enter the morning fasting, nor visit a sick person, nor follow a funeral.""**"
"5098","Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari رضي الله عنهما reports: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan and six of Shawwal, it is as though he fasted the entire year.""**"
"5099","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal, it is as though he fasted all time.""**"
"5100","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts six days consecutively after Fitr, it is as though he fasted the entire year.""**"
"5101","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما and Jabir رضي الله عنه report that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal has fasted the entire year.""**"
"5102","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal emerges from his sins like the day his mother bore him.""**"
"5103","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal, that is the fast of all time.""** He said: I said: For each day ten? He said: > **""Yes.""**"
"5104","Ghannam رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts six after the day of Fitr, it is as though he fasted all time and the year.""**"
"5105","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports: The Prophet ﷺ passed by some Jews who had fasted the day of Ashura, so he said: > **""What is this fast?""** They said: This is the day Allah saved Musa عليه السلام and the Children of Israel from drowning, and drowned Pharaoh therein, and this is the day the ship settled upon Judi, so Nuh عليه السلام and Musa عليه السلام fasted in gratitude to Allah Almighty. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I am more entitled to Musa and more entitled to fast this day.""** He then ordered his Companions to fast."
"5106","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fasting the day of Ashura and said to his Companions: > **""Whoever entered the morning fasting, let him complete his fast, and whoever ate of the breakfast of his family, let him complete the rest of his day.""**"
"5107","Ali رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast Ashura and command it."
"5108","Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah reports: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما, while upon the minbar, say: This is the day of Ashura, so fast it, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded its fast."
"5109","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to the people of a village four farsakhs away, or he said two farsakhs, on the day of Ashura, and ordered whoever had eaten not to eat the rest of his day, and whoever had not eaten to complete his fast."
"5110","Bajah ibn Abdullah ibn Badr reports that his father informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them one day: > **""This is the day of Ashura, so fast it.""** A man from Banu Amr ibn Awf said: O Messenger of Allah, I left my people, some of them fasting and some of them breaking? The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Go to them, and whoever among them was breaking, let him complete his fast.""**"
"5111","Hind ibn Asma al-Aslami رضي الله عنه reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to my people of Aslam and said: > **""Order your people to fast this day, the day of Ashura, and whomever among them you find having eaten in the beginning of his day, let him complete the end of it.""**"
"5112","Yahya ibn Hind ibn Harithah reports, and Hind was among the companions of Hudaybiyah, and his brother whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to order his people to fast Ashura, he is Asma ibn Harithah رضي الله عنه, so Yahya ibn Hind narrated to me from Asma ibn Harithah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him and said: > **""Order your people to fast this day.""** He said: What if I find them having eaten? He said: > **""Then let them complete the end of their day.""**"
"5113","Asma ibn Harithah رضي الله عنه reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me on the day of Ashura and said: > **""Go to your people and order them to fast this day.""** He said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not see myself reaching them before they eat? He said: > **""Then order whoever among them ate to fast the rest of his day.""**"
"5114","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered us regarding the day of Ashura to fast it, and said: > **""It is a day the Jews used to fast.""**"
"5115","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Ashura is the festival of a Prophet who was before you, so fast it yourselves.""**"
"5116","Majzaah ibn Zahir reports from his father: I heard the crier of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Ashura saying: > **""Whoever is fasting today, let him complete his fast, and whoever was not fasting, let him complete what remains.""**"
"5117","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the fast of Ashura, and he himself would not fast it."
"5118","Ulaylah reports from her mother: I said to Amatullah bint Ruzaynah: O Amatullah, did your mother narrate to you that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention the fast of Ashura? She said: Yes, and he would honor it until he would call for his sucklings and the sucklings of his daughter Fatimah رضي الله عنها, then spit in their mouths, and say to the mothers: > **""Do not nurse them until night.""** In one wording: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would honor it until he would call for his children and the nursing children of Fatimah on that day, then spit in their mouths and say to their mothers: > **""Do not nurse them until night.""** And his saliva would suffice them."
"5119","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ mentioned the day of Ashura and honored it, then said to those around him: > **""Whoever among you has not eaten, let him fast this day of his, and whoever among you has eaten, let him fast the rest of his day.""**"
"5120","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه said on the day of Ashura: Fast this day, for the Prophet ﷺ commanded us to fast it."
"5121","Khabbab رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said on the day of Ashura: > **""O people, whoever among you ate, let him not eat the rest of his day, and whoever among you intends the fast, let him fast it.""**"
"5122","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ would not seek the virtue of the fast of one day over another after Ramadan except Ashura."
"5123","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""No day has virtue over a day in fasting except the month of Ramadan and the day of Ashura.""**"
"5124","Said ibn al-Musayyib reports that he heard Muawiyah رضي الله عنه upon the minbar on the day of Ashura say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordering the fast of this day."
"5125","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Fajr on the day of Ashura, and when he finished he said: > **""Whoever among you entered the morning fasting, let him complete his fast, and whoever did not enter the morning fasting, let him not eat anything, for this day is the day of Ashura.""**"
"5126","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Asma ibn Abdullah on the day of Ashura and said: > **""Go to your people; whomever among them you find who has not eaten, let him fast, and whoever ate, let him fast.""**"
"5127","Mabad al-Qurashi reports: The Prophet ﷺ was at Qudayd when a man came to him, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""Have you eaten anything today?""** That was for the day of Ashura. He said: No, except that I drank water. He said: > **""Then do not eat anything until the sun sets, and order those behind you to fast this day.""**"
"5128","Abdullah ibn Abi Sad reports: We entered upon Aidh ibn Amr رضي الله عنه on the day of Ashura, so he said: Milk for them, O boy. The boy rose to a ewe and milked her, then brought it to them. He said to the one on his right: Drink. He said: I am fasting. He said: May Allah accept from us and from you. He then said to the second, who said: I am fasting, so he said the like. He said to the third, who said the like. He said: Are all of you fasting? Soon you will take this day as Ramadan. We used to fast this day before Ramadan was obligated upon us, then when Ramadan was obligated upon us, the fast of Ramadan abrogated the fast of this day, and this day is voluntary, not obligatory, so whoever wills let him fast, and whoever wills let him break. When the people heard that, they all broke."
"5129","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه said: The day of Ashura is not the day people speak of; it was but a day on which the Kaba was covered and the Abyssinians played before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and it would rotate in the year, and the people would come to so-and-so the Jew and ask him, then when the Jew died they came to Zayd ibn Thabit and asked him."
"5130","Ammar رضي الله عنه said: We were ordered to fast Ashura before Ramadan was revealed, then when Ramadan was revealed we were not ordered."
"5131","Qays ibn Abd reports: I frequented Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه for a year, and I never saw him praying Duha, nor saw him fasting a voluntary day except the day of Ashura."
"5132","Abdulaziz ibn Said reports from his father, Uthman ibn Matar, who had companionship, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Rajab is a mighty month in which Allah multiplies good deeds, so whoever fasts a day of Rajab, it is as though he fasted a year, and whoever fasts seven days of it, seven doors of Hell are closed from him, and whoever fasts eight days of it, eight doors of Paradise are opened for him, and whoever fasts ten days of it, he shall not ask Allah for anything except that He gives it to him, and whoever fasts fifteen days of it, a caller in heaven calls: You have been forgiven what has passed, so resume deeds. And whoever increases, Allah increases for him. And in Rajab Allah carried Nuh in the ship, so he fasted Rajab and ordered those with him to fast, and the ship sailed with them seven more months, that is the day of Ashura when it descended upon Judi, so Nuh and those with him and the wild beasts fasted in gratitude to Allah Almighty. And on the day of Ashura Allah parted the sea for the Children of Israel, and on the day of Ashura Allah Most High accepted the repentance of Adam and of the city of Yunus, and therein Ibrahim was born.""** Blessings upon Nuh, Adam, Ibrahim, and Yunus عليه السلام."
"5133","Anas رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""The sea was parted for the Children of Israel on the day of Ashura.""**"
"5134","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fast the day of Ashura and differ therein from the Jews; fast a day before it or a day after it.""**"
"5135","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ ordered fasting Ashura on the tenth day."
"5136","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever expands upon his family on the day of Ashura, Allah shall expand upon him his entire year.""**"
"5137","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Whoever expands upon his dependents on the day of Ashura shall remain in ease the rest of his year.""**"
"5138","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting the day of Arafah for those at Arafat."
"5139","Al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drink a beverage on the day of Arafah."
"5140","Ata al-Khurasani reports that Abdurrahman ibn Abi Bakr رضي الله عنهما entered upon Aishah رضي الله عنها on the day of Arafah while she was fasting and water was being sprinkled upon her, so Abdurrahman said to her: Break your fast! She said: Shall I break while I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The fast of the day of Arafah expiates the year before it?!""**"
"5141","Sahl ibn Sad رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts the day of Arafah shall be forgiven two consecutive years.""**"
"5142","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts the day of Arafah shall be forgiven a year before him and a year behind him, and whoever fasts Ashura shall be forgiven a year.""**"
"5143","Masruq reports that he entered upon Aishah رضي الله عنها on the day of Arafah and said: Give me drink. Aishah said: O boy, give him honey to drink. She then said: And are you not fasting, O Masruq? He said: No, for I fear it is the day of sacrifice. Aishah said: It is not that; Arafah is but the day the Imam defines, and the day of sacrifice is the day the Imam sacrifices. Did you not hear, O Masruq, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would equate it to a thousand days?!"
"5144","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fasts the day of Arafah shall have expiation of two years, and whoever fasts a day of Muharram shall have for each day thirty days.""**"
"5145","Said ibn Jubayr reports: A man asked Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما about the fast of the day of Arafah, so he said: When we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ we would equate it to the fast of two years."
"5146","It is reported from Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about fasting the Day of Arafah. > **""It expiates the year in which you are and the year after it.""**"
"5147","Ikrimah ibn Khalid reported from an `arif of Quraysh who reported from his father that he heard from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Whoever fasts Ramadan and Shawwal and Wednesday and Thursday will enter Paradise.""**"
"5148","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts the Day of Arafah, it will be an expiation of two years, and whoever fasts a day of Muharram, for every day there are thirty days.""**"
"5149","It is reported from Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts a day of Muharram, for every day there are thirty good deeds.""**"
"5150","Jundub ibn Sufyan رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say. > **""The best prayer after the obligatory is prayer in the depth of the night, and the best fasting after Ramadan is the Month of Allah which you call Muharram.""**"
"5151","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts three days of a sacred month, Thursday and Friday and Saturday, there will be recorded for him the worship of sixty years.""**"
"5152","Kharashah ibn al-Hurr reported that he saw Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه striking the palms of men concerning fasting in Rajab until they placed them in food, saying Rajab and what is Rajab, Rajab was only a month which the people of Jahiliyyah used to venerate, then when Islam came it was abandoned."
"5153","Abdul Aziz ibn Sa`id reported from his father, and he was a Companion, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Rajab is a mighty month in which Allah multiplies good deeds. Whoever fasts a day of Rajab, it is as if he fasted a year. Whoever fasts seven days of it, seven gates of Hell are closed from him. Whoever fasts eight days of it, eight gates of Paradise are opened for him. Whoever fasts ten days of it, he does not ask Allah for anything except that He gives it to him. Whoever fasts fifteen days of it, a caller calls in the heaven: you have been forgiven what has passed, so resume deeds. And whoever increases, Allah increases for him. In Rajab Allah carried Nuh عليه السلام in the Ark, so he fasted Rajab and commanded those with him to fast.""**"
"5154","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not complete the fast of a month after Ramadan except Rajab and Sha`ban."
"5155","Anas ibn Sirin reported that they came to Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه on a Thursday and he called for his table and invited them to lunch, so some of the people ate and some refrained. Then they came to him on a Monday and he did the same, calling for his table and inviting them to lunch, so some ate and some refrained. Anas said to them, perhaps you are Monday people, perhaps you are Thursday people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast and not break until we would say there is no intention in the soul of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to break this year, then he would break and not fast until we would say there is no intention in his soul to fast this year. And the most beloved fast to him was in Sha`ban."
"5156","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ would fast all of Sha`ban. She said, I said, O Messenger of Allah, is Sha`ban the most beloved month to you to fast. He said. > **""Allah writes the death of every soul in that year, so I love that my term comes to me while I am fasting.""**"
"5157","Sahl ibn Sa`d رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast until we would say he will not break, and he would break until we would say he will not fast. And most of his fasting was in Sha`ban."
"5158","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to join Sha`ban with Ramadan."
"5159","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to join Sha`ban with Ramadan."
"5160","Abu Tha`labah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast Sha`ban and Ramadan, joining them."
"5161","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast three days of every month, and sometimes he would delay that until the fast of the year gathered upon him, and sometimes he would delay it until he fasted in Sha`ban."
"5162","Abu Malik al-Ash`ari رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""In Paradise there are chambers whose outside is seen from their inside and whose inside is seen from their outside. Allah has prepared them for whoever feeds food and softens speech and continues fasting and prays while the people are asleep.""**"
"5163","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said, and he clenched his fist. > **""Whoever fasts the entire time, Hell will be narrowed upon him like this.""**"
"5164","Abu Qays, the freedman of Amr, reported that Amr رضي الله عنه used to fast continuously without break."
"5165","Mujahid reported that he and Yahya ibn Ja`dah entered upon a man of the Ansar from the Companions of the Messenger ﷺ who said that a freedwoman of Banu Abd al-Muttalib was mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ, that she stood the night and fasted the day. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""But I sleep and pray and break fast. Whoever follows me is from me, and whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not from me. Every deed has zeal and then lull. Whoever whose lull is toward innovation has strayed, and whoever whose lull is toward my Sunnah has been guided.""**"
"5166","Asma bint Yazid رضي الله عنها reported that drink was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and it was passed around the people, and among them was a fasting man. When it reached him he said to him, drink. It was said, O Messenger of Allah, he does not break, he fasts the entire time. He said. > **""He who fasts forever has not fasted.""**"
"5167","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said. > **""He who fasts forever has not fasted.""**"
"5168","Abdullah ibn Sufyan رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said. > **""He who fasts forever has not fasted.""**"
"5169","Amr ibn Salamah reported that Ibn Mas`ud رضي الله عنه was asked about fasting the entire time and he disliked it and said three days of every month."
"5170","Sadaqah al-Dimashqi reported that a man came to Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما asking him about fasting. He said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say. > **""Among the best fasting is the fasting of my brother Dawud عليه السلام. He would fast a day and break a day.""**"
"5171","Mu`adh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah outside Ramadan is distanced from the Fire by one hundred years, the journey of a trained swift horse.""**"
"5172","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah places between him and the Fire a trench as between the heaven and the earth.""**"
"5173","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""The fasting of a man in the path of Allah distances him from Hell by a journey of seventy years.""**"
"5174","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. > **""Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah places between him and the Fire a trench as between the heaven and the earth.""** In another narration, seventy autumns."
"5175","Amr ibn Abasah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, the Fire is distanced from him by a journey of one hundred years.""**"
"5176","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said. > **""Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah distances his face from the Fire by a journey of one hundred years, the gallop of a swift trained horse.""**"
"5177","Utbah ibn Abd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah as an obligation, Allah distances Hell from him as between the heavens and the seven earths. And whoever fasts a day voluntarily, Allah distances Hell from him by a journey of what is between the heaven and the earth.""**"
"5178","Abdullah ibn Sufyan al-Azdi رضي الله عنه, and he was from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, said that there is no man who fasts a day in the path of Allah except that Allah distances him from the Fire by one hundred years. Habib asked Abu Bishr, two hundred years. Abu Bishr said to Uthamah ibn Qays, I had thought that. Abdullah ibn Sufyan said, I only narrate to you what I heard, I do not narrate to you what you narrate to me."
"5179","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""The best of the warriors in the path of Allah is their servant, then the one who brings them news, and the most special of them in station with Allah is the fasting one.""**"
"5180","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. > **""Nuh عليه السلام fasted the entire time except the Day of Fitr and Adha. Dawud عليه السلام fasted half the time. Ibrahim عليه السلام fasted three days of every month, fasting the entire time and breaking the entire time.""**"
"5181","Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah reported that food was brought to Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه and he invited two men to it. One of them said, I am fasting. He said, what fasting do you fast. Were it not for my dislike to add or omit, I would have narrated to you the hadith of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the Bedouin came to him with a rabbit, but send for Ammar. Ammar came and he said, were you witness the day the Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a rabbit. He said, yes. I saw blood in it, so he said, eat it. He said, I am fasting. He said, what fasting do you fast. He said, the beginning of the month and its end. He said. > **""If you are fasting, then fast the thirteenth and the fourteenth and the fifteenth.""**"
"5182","Musa ibn Talhah reported that Umar رضي الله عنه said to Abu Dharr and Ammar and Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنهم, do you remember the day we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in such and such place and a Bedouin came to him with a rabbit in which was blood, and he commanded us and we ate and he did not eat. They said, yes. He said to him, draw near and eat. He said, I am fasting, I fast three days of the month, its beginning and its end, as is easy for me. Umar said, do you know what the Prophet ﷺ commanded him. They said, he commanded him to fast the thirteenth and fourteenth and fifteenth. Umar said, thus said the Prophet ﷺ."
"5183","Musa ibn Talhah reported that he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه while he was giving lunch to the people, and a man passed by him, or a man greeted him, and Umar said to him, come. He said, I am fasting. He said, what part of the month do you fast. He said, of every month its beginning and its middle. Umar said, call Abdullah ibn Mas`ud and Ubayy ibn Ka`b for me, naming men from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. They came and he said, do you remember the day a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a rabbit in such and such valley. They said, yes, and he mentioned the like of it."
"5184","Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir reported from a Bedouin who said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. > **""Fasting the month of patience and three days of every month removes the rancor of the chest.""**"
"5185","Qurrah ibn Iyas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Fasting three days of every month is fasting the entire time and breaking it.""**"
"5186","Hunaydah al-Khuza`i reported from his mother who said she entered upon Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها and asked her about fasting. She said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command me to fast three days of every month, the first of them Monday and Friday and Thursday."
"5187","Ali رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said. > **""Fasting the month of patience and three days removes the rancor of the chest.""**"
"5188","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Fasting the month of patience and three days of every month removes the rancor of the chest.""**"
"5189","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about fasting, and he was distracted from him. Abdullah ibn Mas`ud said to him, fast Ramadan and three days of every month. He said, I seek refuge with Allah from you, O Abdullah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""What do you want. Fast all of Ramadan and three days of every month.""**"
"5190","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ about fasting. He said. > **""Upon you is the white days and three days of every month.""**"
"5191","Abu al-Ala reported that they were at Mirbad when a Bedouin came to them with a piece of hide and said, see what is in it. There was a letter from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to Banu Zuhayr ibn Uqaysh, a clan of Ukl. > **""If you establish prayer and give zakah and render a fifth of what you gain as booty and the share of the Prophet and the select, you are safe with the security of Allah.""** He said, I said, did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, I heard him say. > **""The month of patience and three days of every month removes the rancor of the chest.""** They asked about him and it was said, this is al-Namir ibn Tawlab."
"5192","A man of Banu Sulaym reported that he sat in Mirbad when a Bedouin came with milk from his camels and settled it with them, and his camels crowded them, so they rose from their sitting, then a second time they crowded them. A man of the people said, I see you are mad. He said, I am not mad, and with me is a letter from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He brought it out as a piece of hide and they read it, and in it was. > **""Fasting the month of patience and three days of every month removes the rancor of the chest.""** They said, did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ write this to you. He said, I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote it for me."
"5193","Kahmas al-Hilali رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and stayed with him, then left him, then came to him after a year and said, O Messenger of Allah, do you not know me. He said. > **""No.""** He said, I am the one who was with you last year. He said. > **""What changed you after me.""** He said, I have not eaten food since I parted from you. He said. > **""Who commanded you to torment yourself. Fast a day of the month.""** He said, increase me. He increased him until he said. > **""Fast three days of the month.""**"
"5194","Maymunah bint Sa`d رضي الله عنها reported that she said, O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling about fasting. He said. > **""Three days of every month, whoever is able to fast them, for every day expiates ten sins, and purifies from sin as water purifies a garment.""**"
"5195","Wathilah رضي الله عنه used to fast Monday and Thursday and he used to say the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to fast them and say. > **""Deeds are presented therein to Allah, Blessed and Exalted.""**"
"5196","Abdullah ibn Mas`ud رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would fast Monday and Thursday."
"5197","Abu Rafi` رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would fast Monday and Thursday."
"5198","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said. > **""Do not fast the Day of Saturday except in an obligation, and even if you do not find except the bark of a tree, then break upon it.""**"
"5199","Kurayb reported that people sent him to Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها to ask her which days the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted most. She said, Saturday and Sunday, and he says. > **""They are two festival days of the polytheists, so I love to oppose them.""**"
"5200","Ubayd al-A`raj reported from his grandmother that she entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was having lunch, and that was the Day of Saturday. He said to her. > **""Come and eat.""** She said, I am fasting. He said. > **""Did you fast yesterday.""** She said, no. He said. > **""Eat, for fasting the Day of Saturday is neither for you nor against you.""**"
"5201","Umayr ibn Jubayr, the freedman of Kharijah, reported that the woman who asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about fasting the Day of Saturday narrated to him that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about that and he said. > **""Neither for you nor against you.""**"
"5202","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts Wednesday and Thursday, there will be written for him exemption from the Fire.""**"
"5203","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ the like of it. > **""Whoever fasts Wednesday and Thursday, there will be written for him exemption from the Fire.""**"
"5204","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever fasts Wednesday and Thursday and Friday, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise whose outside is seen from its inside and whose inside is seen from its outside.""**"
"5205","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say. > **""Whoever fasts Wednesday and Thursday and Friday, Allah will build for him a palace in Paradise of pearl and ruby and peridot, and there will be written for him exemption from the Fire.""**"
"5206","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. > **""Whoever fasts the Day of Wednesday and Thursday and Friday, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise whose outside is seen from its inside and whose inside is seen from its outside.""**"
"5207","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. > **""Whoever fasts Wednesday and Thursday and the Day of Friday, then gives charity on the Day of Friday, little or much, he will be forgiven every sin he did until he becomes as on the day his mother bore him from sins.""**"
"5208","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Do not fast the Day of Friday alone.""**"
"5209","Bashir ibn al-Khasasiyah رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, shall I fast the Day of Friday and not speak to anyone that day. He said. > **""Do not fast the Day of Friday except among days of which it is one of them. As for not speaking to anyone, by my life, to speak and enjoin good and forbid evil is better than to be silent.""**"
"5210","Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari رضي الله عنهما reported that they entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Friday while before him was food from which he was eating. He said. > **""Draw near and eat of this food.""** They said, we are fasting, O Messenger of Allah. He said. > **""Did you fast yesterday.""** They said, no. He said. > **""Do you intend to fast tomorrow.""** They said, no. He said. > **""Draw near and eat, for the Day of Friday is not fasted alone, taken as a festival.""**"
"5211","Amir ibn Ludayn al-Ash`ari رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. > **""The Day of Friday is your festival, so do not fast it unless you fast before it or after it.""**"
"5212","Ibn Sirin reported that Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه would enliven the night of Friday and fast its day. Salman رضي الله عنه came to him, and the Prophet ﷺ had made brotherhood between them, and slept with him. Abu al-Darda intended to stand his night, so Salman rose to him and did not leave him until he slept and broke fast. Abu al-Darda came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ said. > **""Uwaymir, Salman knows more than you. Do not single out the night of Friday with prayer nor its day with fasting.""**"
"5213","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he never saw the Prophet ﷺ fasting on a Friday ever."
"5214","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he never saw the Prophet ﷺ breaking fast on the Day of Friday ever."
"5215","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that he never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ break fast on the Day of Friday ever."
"5216","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said. > **""Whoever prays Friday and fasts its day and visits a sick one and witnesses a funeral and witnesses a marriage, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"5217","Abu Sa`id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ who said. > **""Winter is the spring of the believer.""**"
"5218","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Fasting in winter is the cold booty.""**"
"5219","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""A woman does not fast a single day while her husband is present except with his permission, except Ramadan.""**"
"5220","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whatever woman fasts without the permission of her husband, then he desires something from her and she refuses him, Allah writes upon her three of the major sins.""**"
"5221","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave four counsels concerning blessing, sin, delayed provision, and fasting as a guest: > **""Whoever Allah clothes with a blessing, let him increase in praise of Allah; whoever commits many sins, let him seek Allah's forgiveness; whoever finds his provision delayed, let him increase in saying: there is no power and no strength except through Allah; and whoever stays with a people as a guest, let him not fast except with their permission.""**"
"5222","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that a woman visited her, and when food was brought to her she said she was fasting. The Prophet ﷺ asked about the nature of her fast, and when it proved to be voluntary, he instructed her to break it: > **""Is it a make-up fast of Ramadan? ... Then break your fast.""**"
"5223","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded a voluntary faster to respond to his Muslim brother's wish that he break his fast, exempting three obligatory categories: > **""When one of you visits his Muslim brother and he wishes him to break his fast, let him break it, unless his fast is that of Ramadan, or a make-up of Ramadan, or a vow.""**"
"5224","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما would stipulate three conditions for anyone who wished to accompany him on a journey: that he not ride with them upon a weak camel, that he not dispute with them over the call to prayer, and that he not fast except with their permission."
"5225","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the angels' supplication for a fasting person who sits with people while they eat, lasting until he breaks his fast: > **""When a fasting man sits with a people while they are eating, the angels invoke blessings upon him until the fasting person breaks his fast.""**"
"5226","Shaddad ibn Aws رضي الله عنه wept and explained that a statement he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had made him weep, concerning hidden associationism and hidden desire. When asked whether his Ummah would associate partners after him, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed that it would be through ostentation, and he defined the hidden desire through the abandonment of the fast: > **""What I fear most for my Ummah is associationism and hidden desire ... Yes; they will not worship a sun, nor a moon, nor a stone, nor an idol, but they will show off with their deeds. And hidden desire is that one of them wakes in the morning fasting, then a desire from among his desires presents itself to him, so he abandons his fast.""**"
"5227","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that Aishah رضي الله عنها and Hafsah رضي الله عنها woke fasting, then food was gifted to them and they broke their fast. When the Prophet ﷺ came, one of them asked him about it, and he commanded a make-up: > **""Make up a day in its place.""**"
"5228","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه reports that Aishah رضي الله عنها and Hafsah رضي الله عنها, while both fasting, were given a gift and ate from it. When they mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, he commanded a make-up and forbade repetition: > **""Make up a day in its place, and do not do it again.""**"
"5229","Umm Hani bint Abi Talib رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited her on the day of the conquest of Makkah while she was fasting. She brought him a cup of milk, he drank from it, then asked her to drink. When she mentioned her fast, he asked whether it was a make-up fast, and upon learning it was voluntary, he told her to drink, so she drank: > **""Drink ... Is it a make-up fast? ... Then drink.""**"
"5230","Thawban رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fasting outside Ramadan when vomiting overcame him, so he performed ablution and broke his fast. When questioned about it, he affirmed the reason, and on the following day he explained his fasting as a replacement for the previous day's breaking: > **""Yes, but I vomited, so I broke my fast ... This day is in place of my breaking the fast yesterday.""**"
"5231","Abu Talhah رضي الله عنه would wake in the morning voluntarily fasting, then he would come to his family and ask whether they had anything to eat."
"5232","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that some fasters gain nothing but hunger and thirst, and some night worshippers gain nothing but sleeplessness: > **""Many a fasting person has nothing from his fasting but hunger and thirst, and many a night-praying person has nothing from his standing but sleeplessness.""**"
"5233","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered him to announce during the days of Mina, meaning the Days of Tashriq, that fasting is not permitted in them: > **""They are days of eating and drinking, and there is no fast in them.""**"
"5234","In another narration concerning the same announcement, the Prophet ﷺ addressed Sa'd رضي الله عنه with the command to proclaim it in Mina: > **""O Sa'd, stand and proclaim in Mina.""**"
"5235","Abu al-Sha'tha reports that they visited Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما on the middle day of the Days of Tashriq when food was brought. The people came forward while a son of his withdrew, claiming to be fasting, so Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reminded him of the Prophet's ﷺ description of those days: > **""They are days of eating and remembrance.""**"
"5236","Yunus ibn Shaddad رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on the Days of Tashriq: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on the Days of Tashriq.""**"
"5237","Hubaybah bint Shariq رضي الله عنها reports that while she was with her father, Budayl ibn Warqa appeared upon al-Adba, the mount of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, preparing its saddle and proclaiming the Prophet's ﷺ order for those fasting to break their fast because of the nature of those days: > **""Whoever is fasting, let him break his fast, for they are days of eating and drinking.""**"
"5238","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on days of the year including the Day of Fitr, the Day of Sacrifice, and the three Days of Tashriq: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade six days of the year: the Day of Fitr, the Day of Sacrifice, and the three Days of Tashriq.""**"
"5239","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on six days of the year, comprising the two festivals, the Days of Tashriq, and the doubtful day concerning Ramadan: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on six days of the year: the Day of Sacrifice, the Day of Fitr, the Days of Tashriq, and the day concerning which there is doubt whether it is part of Ramadan.""**"
"5240","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a crier to proclaim that those days are not for fasting, describing them as days of eating, drinking, and bi'al, which means marital relations: > **""Do not fast these days, for they are days of eating, drinking, and bi'al.""**"
"5241","In a narration in al-Awsat and al-Kabir, it is reported that the Prophet ﷺ dispatched Budayl ibn Warqa with that proclamation concerning the Days of Tashriq."
"5242","Umm al-Harith bint Ayyash رضي الله عنها reports that she saw Budayl ibn Warqa upon a camel, going among the people and announcing the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ command not to fast those days because of their nature: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands you not to fast these days, for they are days of eating and drinking.""**"
"5243","Ma'mar ibn Abdillah al-Adawi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to announce among the people at Mina the ruling of the Days of Tashriq: > **""The Days of Tashriq are days of eating and drinking.""**"
"5244","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he forbade fasting on three days: the hastened Day of Tarwiyah, the Day of Sacrifice, and the Day of Fitr: > **""He forbade fasting on three days: the hastening of the Day of Tarwiyah, the Day of Sacrifice, and the Day of Fitr.""**"
"5245","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the Days of Tashriq: > **""The Days of Tashriq are days of eating and drinking.""**"
"5246","Usamah al-Hudhali رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man upon a red camel during the days of Mina, who announced to the people that those days are not for fasting: > **""O people, they are days of eating and drinking, so do not fast.""**"
"5247","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that trustworthy men testified before him, the most pleasing of them to him being Umar رضي الله عنه, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on the Day of Fitr and the Day of Sacrifice: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on the Day of Fitr and the Day of Sacrifice.""**"
